  ## Description  

  `res.send()` in `lib/response.js` declares `const type` directly inside a `switch` case clause without wrapping the case body in braces `{}`.                           

  Lexical declarations (`const`/`let`) inside a `switch` case without a block are scoped to the **entire** `switch` statement, not just that case. This means `type` is   
  technically accessible (but in the Temporal Dead Zone) from every other `case` branch — which can cause a `ReferenceError` if any other case tries to reference it
  before initialization. This is exactly the pattern the ESLint `no-case-declarations` rule guards against.                                                               

  ## Location

  `lib/response.js`, line 137

  ## Current Code

  ```js
  case 'string':
    encoding = 'utf8';                                                                                                                                                    
    const type = this.get('Content-Type');

    if (typeof type === 'string') {
      this.set('Content-Type', setCharset(type, 'utf-8'));
    } else {                                                                                                                                                              
      this.type('html');
    }                                                                                                                                                                     
    break;
```       

  ## Suggested Fix   

  Wrap the case body in braces to properly scope the const declaration:                                                                                                   

  ```js
case 'string': {                                                                                                                                                        
    encoding = 'utf8';
    const type = this.get('Content-Type');

    if (typeof type === 'string') {
      this.set('Content-Type', setCharset(type, 'utf-8'));
    } else {                                                                                                                                                              
      this.type('html');
    }                                                                                                                                                                     
    break;        
  }
```

  Notes

  - The rest of the switch block uses var (function-scoped), so only this case is affected.                                                                               
  - Happy to submit a PR for this fix if the team agrees.

